# rota: other boxes that hold a credential this one does not.
#
# Copy to $CLAUDE_FAILOVER_HOME/peers (default ~/.config/claude-failover/peers).
# Machine-local, like `accounts` - it names your machines, so it never belongs
# in the repo. Absent (the default) means: no peers, no ssh, exactly today's
# behaviour.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. A laptop typically holds ONE of the pool's credentials, so
# `rota accounts` there prints `-` in every quota column for the other seats and
# annotates them `[quota none]`. The code is right about the input it has: no
# stored credential, no token, no fetch, nothing to show.
#
# The tempting fix is to copy the credentials here. DO NOT. An OAuth refresh
# token is SINGLE-USE: two boxes holding one account's credential means whichever
# rotates first invalidates the other, the loser 401s, and the CLI hollows its
# file into a husk. That is the 2026-08-07 incident. A DISPLAY problem must never
# be paid for with a second copy of a credential.
#
# So instead this reads the NUMBERS from the box that legitimately holds the
# credential: one ssh, read-only, `rota accounts --json --no-refresh`, nothing
# moves. Rows filled in this way are marked `[via <host>]` in NOTES, with their
# age when the peer's own number is not fresh, and `--json` carries
# quota_source + quota_measured_at so no borrowed number can pass for a local one.
#
# FORMAT: one ssh destination per line. Blank lines and anything after a # are
# ignored. Use the name you would type after `ssh`, so any Host block, jump host
# or LAN/VPN/tunnel fallback you already have for that name is inherited for
# free. A line naming THIS box is skipped rather than dialled.
#
# WHEN A PEER IS CONSULTED: only when at least one row is missing or cached,
# only with the network on (`--no-refresh` still does zero network), and at most
# one round trip per 90s (ROTA_PEER_TTL). The 10s bound (ROTA_PEER_TIMEOUT) is on
# the WHOLE step, however many hosts are listed, not per host. A peer that fails
# is left alone for 300s (ROTA_PEER_FAIL_TTL) so a sleeping box costs one stall
# per five minutes rather than one per command; `rm` the cache file named below
# to retry immediately. ROTA_PEER_TIMEOUT=0 turns the peer step OFF for one run,
# and a knob that is not a plain number of seconds (`10s`) falls back to its
# default rather than costing you the table. The first peer that answers usefully wins; any failure at
# all is silent, and the table degrades to exactly what it printed before.
# `--verbose` explains. State lives in $CLAUDE_FAILOVER_HOME/peer-usage-cache.json.
#
# ⚠️ UPGRADE THE PEER TOO. The peer should run the same rota as this box, or
# newer. Against an older one everything still works, but two things silently
# degrade, and both make a number look better than it is:
#   * NO PER-ROW AGE. Freshness falls back to the payload's generated_at, which
#     is when the peer BUILT the report, not when it measured the seat. A
#     genuinely days-old peer number then outranks a one-hour-old local cache and
#     renders as a bare `[via <host>]` with no age at all.
#   * NO 5H RESET. `five_hour_resets_at` is new, so a borrowed row carries the 5h
#     percentage without the instant it resets.
# Both fields (quota_measured_at, five_hour_resets_at) arrived together; a peer
# that publishes them is a peer that can be trusted to date its own numbers.
#
# Examples:
# always-on-box
# home-server
